  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a strong, mechanistically supported relationship between the complex compound [(2'S,3S,3'R,4S,4'R,5R,10S,13S,14R,17S)-3-[(2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-6-[[(2S,3R,4S,5S)-4,5-dihydroxy-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5R,6R)-5-hydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-4-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxy-3-[(2S,3R,4R,5R,6S)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-methyloxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxyoxan-2-yl]oxymethyl]-3,4,5-trihydroxyoxan-2-yl]oxy-4-(hydroxymethyl)-4,4',10,13,14-pentamethyl-7,11-dioxo-2'-propanoylspiro[1,2,3,5,6,12,15,16-octahydrocyclopenta[a]phenanthrene-17,5'-oxolane]-3'-yl] acetate and the CD276 antigen. This relationship is established through multiple independent pathways, all pointing to WI-38 as a common target. WI-38 is known to be a cell line that expresses CD276, and multiple compounds—Taiwanin E, Cyclo(L-Phe-L-Leu), and 2,5-Imino-2,5,6-Trideoxy-D-Manno-Heptitol—have been shown to interact with CD276 in this cell line. The consistent presence of CD276 as a target across these compounds supports a mechanism of action that is likely related to the inhibition of CD276, possibly through binding or modulation of the antigen. The evidence is robust and aligns with known biological interactions, suggesting that the compound in question may act as a CD276 modulator or inhibitor. The mechanism is not fully elucidated, but the shared target and the presence of multiple compounds with similar target profiles indicate a plausible, mechanistically grounded relationship.",
